.TH V32LUA 1 "September 2026" "v32lua 20260915-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
v32lua \- compile a subset of Lua to Vircon32 assembly and cartridge XML
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B v32lua
.RB [ \-o
.IR output.asm ]
.RB [ \-g ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.IR input.lua " | " cart.p8
.br
.B v32lua
.B \-\-version
.br
.B v32lua
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B v32lua
compiles a purpose-built subset of Lua \(em global and local variables,
functions as first-class values, multiple assignment, the usual
statement/expression language, and a set of Vircon32-specific hardware
intrinsics \(em directly to
.B Vircon32
assembly. There is no bytecode virtual machine and no interpreter
involved at any point: Lua source is lowered straight to native
instructions for the target console's CPU, in the same spirit as
.BR v32c++ (1)
lowering a C++ subset to plain Vircon32 C.
.PP
A compile produces two files: an assembly file
(see
.BR \-o ,
below) and, alongside it, a cartridge XML definition (the same base
name with a
.I .xml
extension) that records the cart's title, version, and any texture,
sound, and tilemap resources declared with
.B \-\-#
hints in the source (see
.B CARTRIDGE HINTS
below). Both are inputs to the ordinary Vircon32 build chain \(em
assembler, then
.BR packrom (1)
\(em which
.B v32lua
does not itself invoke.
.PP
.B v32lua
produces no output at all on success unless a verbosity flag is given
(see
.BR \-v ,
below) \(em this matches the Vircon32 C compiler's and
.BR v32c++ (1)'s
own behavior. A non-zero exit status, and a message on standard
error, indicate a genuine problem: a lexical, syntax, semantic, or
internal compiler error. Lexical, syntax, semantic, and internal
errors are reported with the offending source line reproduced
inline, pointing at the failure.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output.asm"
Write the generated assembly to
.IR output.asm ,
instead of the default. If
.B \-o
is not given at all, the output filename is derived from the input's
own name: its extension is replaced with
.IR .asm ,
so
.I game/main.lua
becomes
.IR game/main.asm .
The companion
.I .xml
cartridge file is always named from this same output path (its own
extension replaced with
.IR .xml ),
not from the input filename, so
.B \-o
also controls where the XML lands.
.TP
.B \-g
Generate a companion
.I output.asm.debug
file mapping relative assembly line offsets back to the original Lua
source line and enclosing function name for each line of generated
code. Intended for step-through debugging of a compiled cart under
.BR v32sim (1),
not for anything read by the assembler or
.BR packrom (1)
itself.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report progress through the compiler's internal pipeline stages
(lexer, preprocessor, parser, semantic analysis, code generation) as
each one begins. No
.B \-v
at all (the default) produces no output on success.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Print additional internal/operational debug information as the
compiler runs. Unrelated to
.BR \-g ;
this is compiler-development output, not a source-to-assembly map for
the resulting cart.
.TP
.B \-w
Suppress all compiler warnings (for example, a missing
.B WAIT
inside a
.B main()
entry point, or a mismatched cart-resource ordering). Errors are
unaffected and still halt compilation.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version, author, and project URL information, and exit
immediately without processing any input.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a brief usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The input was compiled successfully.
.TP
.B 1
No input file was given; an unrecognized or duplicate argument was
given; or a lexical, syntax, semantic, or internal compiler error
occurred while compiling. Details are printed to standard error, and,
for a source-position error, the offending line is reproduced inline.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compile a single file, writing
.I program.asm
and
.IR program.xml :
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32lua \-o program.asm program.lua
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile with the default output name (extension swapped for
.IR .asm ),
watching each pipeline stage run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32lua \-v game.lua
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile with a source-to-assembly debug map, for stepping through the
cart in
.BR v32sim (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32lua \-g \-o build/game.asm src/game.lua
.fi
.RE
.SH ENTRY POINTS
A program must declare at least one of
.B main()
or
.BR game_loop() ;
its absence is a compile error.
.TP
.B game_loop()
If present, the compiler generates a continuous runtime harness: the
console calls
.BR game_loop() ,
issues a
.B WAIT
for the current frame, and repeats indefinitely. This is the usual
entry point for arcade-style games, analogous to
.B TIC()
in TIC-80.
.TP
.B main()
If present instead, the console halts once
.B main()
returns, mimicking a hosted C
.BR main() .
Continuous execution and frame pacing (including any
.B WAIT
instructions) are then the program's own responsibility; the compiler
warns at compile time if it does not find a
.B WAIT
emitted inside
.BR main() .
.TP
.B init()
If present, alongside either of the above, it runs exactly once,
after top-level global RAM allocation and before the main loop
begins.
.SH LANGUAGE NOTES
This section is a brief summary, not a complete reference \(em see the
project's own README, and
.I doc/API.md
for the full hardware intrinsic surface, for the current, authoritative
picture, including known gaps under active development.
.PP
Supported: global and local variables, with a top-level
.B local
promoted to a global slot rather than a doomed stack local (its
storage would otherwise live in a frame that returns before any game
code runs \(em this applies equally to top-level locals pulled in via
.BR \-\-#include );
functions as first-class NaN-boxed values; multiple assignment and
variable swapping without user temporaries;
.B __asm__
(register/stack-preserving) and
.B __rawasm__
(unprotected) inline assembly, both supporting
.I {var_name}
string interpolation of Lua variables; a native, zero-cost hardware
intrinsic surface
.RI ( ioports.gpu.* ", " ioports.spu.* ", " ioports.inp.* ", "
.IR music.*/sfx.* ", " tilemap.* ", " system.* );
a TIC-80- and an (earlier-stage) PICO-8-compatible API layer, selected
per-cartridge with a
.B \-\-#api
hint; and a
.B \-\-#include
textual preprocessor for multi-file projects, resolved before parsing
begins.
.PP
Graphics conveniences in the native API:
.B ioports.gpu.clear()
takes no argument (reuse the current clear color), a preset name
.RB ( \(dqblack\(dq ", " \(dqwhite\(dq ", " \(dqblue\(dq ,
.BR \(dqred\(dq ", " \(dqgreen\(dq ),
a packed
.I 0xAABBGGRR
color (a literal, or a
.B hex()
value when it comes from a variable), or separate components as
.BI clear( r ", " g ", " b " [, " a ])\fR,\fP
each 0\(en255 with alpha defaulting to 255 (opaque) \(em folded to a
constant when all are literals, packed at runtime otherwise.
.BR spr() 's
eighth argument,
.IR blend_mode ,
accepts either a number
.RB ( 0x20 ", " 0x21 ", " 0x22 )
or one of the string literals
.BR \(dqalpha\(dq " (or " \(dqdefault\(dq "), " \(dqadd\(dq ", or " \(dqsubtract\(dq ,
resolved at compile time; for example
.BR "spr(id, x, y, nil, nil, nil, nil, \(dqadd\(dq)" .
.PP
Explicit, permanent deviations from standard Lua, made to suit
Vircon32's freestanding environment: IEEE-754 float32 only, with no
Lua 5.3+ integer/float dual-number type \(em integers above 2^24 are
not exactly representable;
.B print()
requires screen
.B x
and
.B y
as its first two arguments; a bare
.B return
statement is a syntax error (give it a value); and all program
execution must occur inside a function reachable from
.BR main() / game_loop() .
.PP
Also supported: single-quoted and long
.RB ( [[...]] )
string literals, exponent number literals
.RB ( 1e\-3 ),
.B goto
with block-scoped
.B ::labels::
(forward references included), and string-library calls as methods on
string values
.RB ( s:sub(2,\ #s) ", " (\(dqx\(dq):upper() ).
Strings used as table keys compare by content.
.PP
A PICO-8 cartridge compiles directly: given a
.I .p8
file, only its
.B __lua__
section is compiled (line numbers still refer to the
.I .p8
file) and its
.BR __gfx__ ", " __gff__ " and " __map__
sections become the sprite sheet, sprite flags and map. A
.I .lua
program can take those assets from a cart with
.BR \-\-#p8\ \(dqcart.p8\(dq .
.PP
Not yet implemented:
.BR pcall / error / assert ;
.BR string.match / gmatch ", " setmetatable ;
.BR select() ,
and
.B next()
as a callable function;
expansion of a trailing multi-value call inside a table constructor or
argument list
.RB ( {f()} ", " g(f()) " keep only the first value);"
arithmetic coercion of numeric strings;
bitwise operators;
garbage collection (the heap only grows);
the two-argument, explicit-base form of
.BR tonumber() " (an explicit numeric base);"
and a diagnostic for reading, from inside a function, a
.B local
declared in a block lexically outside any function at chunk level
(for example inside a bare
.B do...end
at chunk level) \(em such a read currently compiles silently to an
uninitialized global rather than the value the block appeared to
scope.
.SH CARTRIDGE HINTS
Cartridge metadata and resources are declared with
.B \-\-#
line comments, parsed by the preprocessor stage and used to generate
the companion XML file:
.BR \-\-#version ", " \-\-#title ", " \-\-#api ", " \-\-#texture ,
.BR \-\-#sound ", " \-\-#tilemap ", " \-\-#p8 ", " \-\-#p8rate ", and " \-\-#include .
A
.B \-\-#texture
or
.B \-\-#sound
hint both registers the resource in the XML and binds its name to a
compile-time constant usable directly from Lua; resource IDs are
assigned in source order and are guaranteed to match their position
in the generated XML.
.B \-\-#include \(dqfile.lua\(dq
textually splices another file in before parsing, resolved relative
to the including file, with include-once deduplication and cycle
detection; it performs no scoping transformation of any kind; a
.B local
at the included file's own top level is promoted to a global exactly
as in a monolithic file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I *.lua
Input source, in the Lua subset this tool accepts.
.TP
.I *.p8
A PICO-8 cartridge, compiled directly (see
.BR "LANGUAGE NOTES" ).
.TP
.IR *_pico8.vtex ", " *_sfxNN.vsnd
Generated next to the output for PICO-8 carts: the sprite sheet (plus
color swatches), and the cart's 64
.B __sfx__
entries synthesized into sounds (22050 Hz by default, see
.BR \-\-#p8rate ).
.BR music()
is sequenced from them at run time.
.TP
.IR pico8_tone0.vsnd " .. " pico8_tone7.vsnd
Placeholder tones for PICO-8 programs without sound data, and for TIC-80
carts that use
.BR sfx() / music()
(an existing file of the same name is kept).
.TP
.I *.asm
Generated Vircon32 assembly, ready to be handed to the Vircon32
assembler directly.
.TP
.I *.xml
Generated cartridge definition, named from the assembly output path
(not the input path), referencing any
.I .vtex
and
.I .vsnd
assets registered via cartridge hints. Required by
.BR packrom (1)
alongside the assembled ROM.
.TP
.I *.asm.debug
Optional, with
.BR \-g :
a line-oriented map from generated assembly line offsets back to
original Lua source lines and enclosing function names, for use by a
debugger such as
.BR v32sim (1).
.PP
Texture and sound source assets
.RI ( .png ", " .wav ", and similarly " .vsnd )
referenced by cartridge hints are read directly by
.B v32lua
at compile time and are not themselves an output of this tool.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32c++ (1),
the Vircon32 C compiler and assembler documentation,
.BR v32opt (1)
(a separate, general-purpose Vircon32 assembly optimizer meant to sit
between this tool and the assembler).
.PP
Project repository:
.I https://github.com/wedge1020/v32lua
